.SH "NAME"
pcre2pattern \- PCRE2 pattern syntax
.SH "PCRE2 REGULAR EXPRESSION DETAILS"
.sp
The syntax and semantics of the regular expressions that are supported by PCRE2
are described in detail below. There is a quick\-reference syntax summary in the
\f(CRpcre2syntax\fP
page. PCRE2 tries to match Perl syntax and semantics as closely as it can.
PCRE2 also supports some alternative regular expression syntax that does not
conflict with the Perl syntax in order to provide some compatibility with
regular expressions in Python, .NET, and Oniguruma. There are in addition some
options that enable alternative syntax and semantics that are not the same as
in Perl.
.sp
Perl\(cqs regular expressions are described in its own documentation, and regular
expressions in general are covered in a number of books, some of which have
copious examples. Jeffrey Friedl\(cqs "Mastering Regular Expressions", published
by O\(cqReilly, covers regular expressions in great detail. This description of
PCRE2\(cqs regular expressions is intended as reference material.
.sp
This document discusses the regular expression patterns that are supported by
PCRE2 when its main matching function, pcre2_match(), is used. PCRE2 also
has an alternative matching function, pcre2_dfa_match(), which matches
using a different algorithm that is not Perl\-compatible. Some of the features
discussed below are not available when DFA matching is used. The advantages and
disadvantages of the alternative function, and how it differs from the normal
function, are discussed in the
\f(CRpcre2matching\fP
page.
.SH "EBCDIC CHARACTER CODES"
.sp
Most computers use ASCII or Unicode for encoding characters, and PCRE2 assumes
this by default. However, it can be compiled to run in an environment that uses
the EBCDIC code, which is the case for some IBM mainframe operating systems. In
the sections below, character code values are ASCII or Unicode; in an EBCDIC
environment these characters may have different code values, and there are no
code points greater than 255. Differences in behaviour when PCRE2 is running in
an EBCDIC environment are described in the section EBCDIC ENVIRONMENTS
below, which you can ignore unless you really are in an EBCDIC environment.
.SH "SPECIAL START\-OF\-PATTERN ITEMS"
.sp
A number of options that can be passed to pcre2_compile() can also be set
by special items at the start of a pattern. These are not Perl\-compatible, but
are provided to make these options accessible to pattern writers who are not
able to change the program that processes the pattern. Any number of these
items may appear, but they must all be together right at the start of the
pattern string, and the letters must be in upper case.
.SS "UTF support"
.sp
In the 8\-bit and 16\-bit PCRE2 libraries, characters may be coded either as
single code units, or as multiple UTF\-8 or UTF\-16 code units. UTF\-32 can be
specified for the 32\-bit library, in which case it constrains the character
values to valid Unicode code points. To process UTF strings, PCRE2 must be
built to include Unicode support (which is the default). When using UTF strings
you must either call the compiling function with one or both of the \fBPCRE2_UTF\fP
or \fBPCRE2_MATCH_INVALID_UTF\fP options, or the pattern must start with the special
sequence \f(CR(*UTF)\fP, which is equivalent to setting the relevant \fBPCRE2_UTF\fP. How
setting a UTF mode affects pattern matching is mentioned in several places
below. There is also a summary of features in the
\f(CRpcre2unicode\fP
page.
.sp
Some applications that allow their users to supply patterns may wish to
restrict them to non\-UTF data for security reasons. If the \fBPCRE2_NEVER_UTF\fP
option is passed to pcre2_compile(), \f(CR(*UTF)\fP is not allowed, and its
appearance in a pattern causes an error.
.SS "Unicode property support"
.sp
Another special sequence that may appear at the start of a pattern is \f(CR(*UCP)\fP.
This has the same effect as setting the \fBPCRE2_UCP\fP option: it causes sequences
such as \f(CR\(rsd\fP and \f(CR\(rsw\fP to use Unicode properties to determine character types,
instead of recognizing only characters with codes less than 256 via a lookup
table. If also causes upper/lower casing operations to use Unicode properties
for characters with code points greater than 127, even when UTF is not set.
These behaviours can be changed within the pattern; see the section
INTERNAL OPTION SETTING
below.
.sp
Some applications that allow their users to supply patterns may wish to
restrict them for security reasons. If the \fBPCRE2_NEVER_UCP\fP option is passed to
pcre2_compile(), \f(CR(*UCP)\fP is not allowed, and its appearance in a pattern
causes an error.
.SS "Locking out empty string matching"
.sp
Starting a pattern with \f(CR(*NOTEMPTY)\fP or \f(CR(*NOTEMPTY_ATSTART)\fP has the same effect
as passing the \fBPCRE2_NOTEMPTY\fP or \fBPCRE2_NOTEMPTY_ATSTART\fP option to whichever
matching function is subsequently called to match the pattern. These options
lock out the matching of empty strings, either entirely, or only at the start
of the subject.
.SS "Disabling auto\-possessification"
.sp
If a pattern starts with \f(CR(*NO_AUTO_POSSESS)\fP, it has the same effect as setting
the \fBPCRE2_NO_AUTO_POSSESS\fP option, or calling pcre2_set_optimize() with
a \fBPCRE2_AUTO_POSSESS_OFF\fP directive. This stops PCRE2 from making quantifiers
possessive when what follows cannot match the repeated item. For example, by
default \f(CRa+b\fP is treated as \f(CRa++b\fP. For more details, see the
\f(CRpcre2api\fP
documentation.
.SS "Disabling start\-up optimizations"
.sp
If a pattern starts with \f(CR(*NO_START_OPT)\fP, it has the same effect as setting the
\fBPCRE2_NO_START_OPTIMIZE\fP option, or calling pcre2_set_optimize() with
a \fBPCRE2_START_OPTIMIZE_OFF\fP directive. This disables several optimizations for
quickly reaching "no match" results. For more details, see the
\f(CRpcre2api\fP
documentation.
.SS "Disabling automatic anchoring"
.sp
If a pattern starts with \f(CR(*NO_DOTSTAR_ANCHOR)\fP, it has the same effect as
setting the \fBPCRE2_NO_DOTSTAR_ANCHOR\fP option, or
calling pcre2_set_optimize() with a \fBPCRE2_DOTSTAR_ANCHOR_OFF\fP directive.
This disables optimizations that apply to patterns whose top\-level branches
all start with \f(CR.*\fP (match any number of arbitrary characters). For more details,
see the
\f(CRpcre2api\fP
documentation.
.SS "Disabling JIT compilation"
.sp
If a pattern that starts with \f(CR(*NO_JIT)\fP is successfully compiled, an attempt by
the application to apply the JIT optimization by calling
pcre2_jit_compile() is ignored.
.SS "Setting match resource limits"
.sp
The pcre2_match() function contains a counter that is incremented every
time it goes round its main loop. The caller of pcre2_match() can set a
limit on this counter, which therefore limits the amount of computing resource
used for a match. The maximum depth of nested backtracking can also be limited;
this indirectly restricts the amount of heap memory that is used, but there is
also an explicit memory limit that can be set.
.sp
These facilities are provided to catch runaway matches that are provoked by
patterns with huge matching trees. A common example is a pattern with nested
unlimited repeats applied to a long string that does not match. When one of
these limits is reached, pcre2_match() gives an error return. The limits
can also be set by items at the start of the pattern of the form
.sp
.ss \n[.ss]
.nf
.fam C
(*LIMIT_HEAP=\fId\fP)
(*LIMIT_MATCH=\fId\fP)
(*LIMIT_DEPTH=\fId\fP)
.fam
.fi
.ss \n[.ss] 0
.sp
where \fId\fP is any number of decimal digits. However, the value of the setting must
be less than the value set (or defaulted) by the caller of pcre2_match()
for it to have any effect. In other words, the pattern writer can lower the
limits set by the programmer, but not raise them. If there is more than one
setting of one of these limits, the lower value is used. The heap limit is
specified in kibibytes (units of 1024 bytes).
.sp
Prior to release 10.30, LIMIT_DEPTH was called LIMIT_RECURSION. This name is
still recognized for backwards compatibility.
.sp
The heap limit applies only when the pcre2_match() or
pcre2_dfa_match() interpreters are used for matching. It does not apply
to JIT. The match limit is used (but in a different way) when JIT is being
used, or when pcre2_dfa_match() is called, to limit computing resource
usage by those matching functions. The depth limit is ignored by JIT but is
relevant for DFA matching, which uses function recursion for recursions within
the pattern and for lookaround assertions and atomic groups. In this case, the
depth limit controls the depth of such recursion.
.SS "Newline conventions"
.sp
PCRE2 supports six different conventions for indicating line breaks in
strings: a single CR (carriage return) character, a single LF (linefeed)
character, the two\-character sequence CRLF, any of the three preceding, any
Unicode newline sequence, or the NUL character (binary zero). The
\f(CRpcre2api\fP
page has
further discussion
about newlines, and shows how to set the newline convention when calling
pcre2_compile().
.sp
It is also possible to specify a newline convention by starting a pattern
string with one of the following sequences:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 11
\f(CR(*CR)\fP
.ad l
Carriage return
.TP 11
\f(CR(*LF)\fP
.ad l
Linefeed
.TP 11
\f(CR(*CRLF)\fP
.ad l
Carriage return, followed by linefeed
.TP 11
\f(CR(*ANYCRLF)\fP
.ad l
Any of the three above
.TP 11
\f(CR(*ANY)\fP
.ad l
All Unicode newline sequences
.TP 11
\f(CR(*NUL)\fP
.ad l
The NUL character (binary zero)
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
These override the default and the options given to the compiling function. For
example, on a Unix system where LF is the default newline sequence, the pattern
.sp
.ss \n[.ss]
.nf
.fam C
(*CR)a.b
.fam
.fi
.ss \n[.ss] 0
.sp
changes the convention to CR. That pattern matches \f(CRa\(rsnb\fP because LF is no
longer a newline. If more than one of these settings is present, the last one
is used.
.sp
The newline convention affects where the circumflex and dollar assertions are
true. It also affects the interpretation of the dot metacharacter when
\fBPCRE2_DOTALL\fP is not set, and the behaviour of \f(CR\(rsN\fP when not followed by an
opening brace. However, it does not affect what the \f(CR\(rsR\fP escape sequence
matches. By default, this is any Unicode newline sequence, for Perl
compatibility. However, this can be changed; see the section
Specifying what \f(CR\(rsR\fP matches and the
description of \f(CR\(rsR\fP in the section Newline sequences
below. A change of \f(CR\(rsR\fP setting can be combined with a change of newline
convention.
.SS "Specifying what \f(CR\(rsR\fP matches"
.sp
It is possible to restrict \f(CR\(rsR\fP to match only CR, LF, or CRLF (instead of the
complete set of Unicode line endings) by setting the option \fBPCRE2_BSR_ANYCRLF\fP
at compile time. This effect can also be achieved by starting a pattern with
\f(CR(*BSR_ANYCRLF)\fP. For completeness, \f(CR(*BSR_UNICODE)\fP is also recognized,
corresponding to \fBPCRE2_BSR_UNICODE\fP.
.SH "CHARACTERS AND METACHARACTERS"
.sp
A regular expression is a pattern that is matched against a subject string from
left to right. Most characters stand for themselves in a pattern, and match the
corresponding characters in the subject. As a trivial example, the pattern
.sp
.ss \n[.ss]
.nf
.fam C
The quick brown fox
.fam
.fi
.ss \n[.ss] 0
.sp
matches a portion of a subject string that is identical to itself. When
caseless matching is specified (the \fBPCRE2_CASELESS\fP option or \f(CR(?i)\fP within the
pattern), letters are matched independently of case. Note that there are two
ASCII characters, K and S, that, in addition to their lower case ASCII
equivalents, are case\-equivalent with Unicode \f(CRU+212A\fP (Kelvin sign) and \f(CRU+017F\fP
(long S) respectively when either \fBPCRE2_UTF\fP or \fBPCRE2_UCP\fP is set, unless the
\fBPCRE2_EXTRA_CASELESS_RESTRICT\fP option is in force (either passed to
pcre2_compile() or set by \f(CR(*CASELESS_RESTRICT)\fP or \f(CR(?r)\fP within the
pattern). If the \fBPCRE2_EXTRA_TURKISH_CASING\fP option is in force (either passed
to pcre2_compile() or set by \f(CR(*TURKISH_CASING)\fP within the pattern), then
the \*(Aqi\*(Aq letters are matched according to Turkish and Azeri languages.
.sp
The power of regular expressions comes from the ability to include wild cards,
character classes, alternatives, and repetitions in the pattern. These are
encoded in the pattern by the use of \fImetacharacters\fP, which do not stand
for themselves but instead are interpreted in some special way.
.sp
There are two different sets of metacharacters: those that are recognized
anywhere in the pattern except within square brackets, and those that are
recognized within square brackets. Outside square brackets, the metacharacters
are as follows:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rs\fP
.ad l
general escape character with several uses
.TP 7
\f(CR^\fP
.ad l
assert start of string (or line, in multiline mode)
.TP 7
\f(CR$\fP
.ad l
assert end of string (or line, in multiline mode)
.TP 7
\f(CR.\fP
.ad l
match any character except newline (by default)
.TP 7
\f(CR[\fP
.ad l
start character class definition
.TP 7
\f(CR|\fP
.ad l
start of alternative branch
.TP 7
\f(CR(\fP
.ad l
start group or control verb
.TP 7
\f(CR)\fP
.ad l
end group or control verb
.TP 7
\f(CR*\fP
.ad l
0 or more quantifier
.TP 7
\f(CR+\fP
.ad l
1 or more quantifier; also "possessive quantifier"
.TP 7
\f(CR?\fP
.ad l
0 or 1 quantifier; also quantifier minimizer
.TP 7
\f(CR{\fP
.ad l
potential start of min/max quantifier
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
Brace characters \f(CR{\fP and \f(CR}\fP are also used to enclose data for constructions such
as \f(CR\(rsg{2}\fP or \f(CR\(rsk{\fP\fIname\fP\f(CR}\fP. In almost all uses of braces, space and/or horizontal
tab characters that follow \f(CR{\fP or precede \f(CR}\fP are allowed and are ignored. In the
case of quantifiers, they may also appear before or after the comma. The
exception to this is \f(CR\(rsu{...}\fP which is an ECMAScript compatibility feature
that is recognized only when the \fBPCRE2_EXTRA_ALT_BSUX\fP option is set. ECMAScript
does not ignore such white space; it causes the item to be interpreted as
literal.
.sp
Part of a pattern that is in square brackets is called a "character class". In
a character class the only metacharacters are:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rs\fP
.ad l
general escape character
.TP 7
\f(CR^\fP
.ad l
negate the class, but only if the first character
.TP 7
\f(CR\-\fP
.ad l
indicates character range
.TP 7
\f(CR[\fP
.ad l
POSIX character class (if followed by POSIX syntax)
.TP 7
\f(CR]\fP
.ad l
terminates the character class
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
If a pattern is compiled with the \fBPCRE2_EXTENDED\fP option, most white space in
the pattern, other than in a character class, within a \f(CR\(rsQ...\(rsE\fP sequence, or
between a \f(CR#\fP outside a character class and the next newline, inclusive, is
ignored. An escaping backslash can be used to include a white space or a #
character as part of the pattern. If the \fBPCRE2_EXTENDED_MORE\fP option is set, the
same applies, but in addition unescaped space and horizontal tab characters are
ignored inside a character class. Note: only these two characters are ignored,
not the full set of pattern white space characters that are ignored outside a
character class. Option settings can be changed within a pattern; see the
section INTERNAL OPTION SETTING
below.
.sp
The following sections describe the use of each of the metacharacters.
.SH "BACKSLASH"
.sp
The backslash character has several uses. Firstly, if it is followed by a
character that is not a digit or a letter, it takes away any special meaning
that character may have. This use of backslash as an escape character applies
both inside and outside character classes.
.sp
For example, if you want to match a \f(CR*\fP character, you must write \f(CR\(rs*\fP in the
pattern. This escaping action applies whether or not the following character
would otherwise be interpreted as a metacharacter, so it is always safe to
precede a non\-alphanumeric with backslash to specify that it stands for itself.
In particular, if you want to match a backslash, you write \f(CR\(rs\(rs\fP.
.sp
Only ASCII digits and letters have any special meaning after a backslash. All
other characters (in particular, those whose code points are greater than 127)
are treated as literals.
.sp
If you want to treat all characters in a sequence as literals, you can do so by
putting them between \f(CR\(rsQ\fP and \f(CR\(rsE\fP. Note that this includes white space even when
the \fBPCRE2_EXTENDED\fP option is set so that most other white space is ignored. The
behaviour is different from Perl in that $ and @ are handled as literals in
\f(CR\(rsQ...\(rsE\fP sequences in PCRE2, whereas in Perl, $ and @ cause variable
interpolation. Also, Perl does "double\-quotish backslash interpolation" on any
backslashes between \f(CR\(rsQ\fP and \f(CR\(rsE\fP which, its documentation says, "may lead to
confusing results". PCRE2 treats a backslash between \f(CR\(rsQ\fP and \f(CR\(rsE\fP just like any
other character. Note the following examples:
.TS
allbox tab(:);
ltB ltB ltB.
T{
.sp
Pattern
T}:T{
.sp
PCRE2 matches
T}:T{
.sp
Perl matches
T}
.T&
lt lt lt.
T{
.sp
\f(CR\(rsQabc$xyz\(rsE\fP
T}:T{
.sp
\f(CRabc$xyz\fP
T}:T{
.sp
\f(CRabc\fP followed by the contents of \f(CR$xyz\fP
T}
T{
.sp
\f(CR\(rsQabc\(rs$xyz\(rsE\fP
T}:T{
.sp
\f(CRabc\(rs$xyz\fP
T}:T{
.sp
\f(CRabc\(rs$xyz\fP
T}
T{
.sp
\f(CR\(rsQabc\(rsE\(rs$\(rsQxyz\(rsE\fP
T}:T{
.sp
\f(CRabc$xyz\fP
T}:T{
.sp
\f(CRabc$xyz\fP
T}
T{
.sp
\f(CR\(rsQA\(rsB\(rsE\fP
T}:T{
.sp
\f(CRA\(rsB\fP
T}:T{
.sp
\f(CRA\(rsB\fP
T}
T{
.sp
\f(CR\(rsQ\(rs\(rsE\fP
T}:T{
.sp
\f(CR\(rs\fP
T}:T{
.sp
\f(CR\(rs\(rsE\fP
T}
.TE
.sp
.sp
The \f(CR\(rsQ...\(rsE\fP sequence is recognized both inside and outside character classes.
An isolated \f(CR\(rsE\fP that is not preceded by \f(CR\(rsQ\fP is ignored. If \f(CR\(rsQ\fP is not followed
by \f(CR\(rsE\fP later in the pattern, the literal interpretation continues to the end of
the pattern (that is, \f(CR\(rsE\fP is assumed at the end). If the isolated \f(CR\(rsQ\fP is inside
a character class, this causes an error, because the character class is then
not terminated by a closing square bracket.
.sp
Another difference from Perl is that any appearance of \f(CR\(rsQ\fP or \f(CR\(rsE\fP inside what
might otherwise be a quantifier causes PCRE2 not to recognize the sequence as a
quantifier. Perl recognizes a quantifier if (redundantly) either of the numbers
is inside \f(CR\(rsQ...\(rsE\fP, but not if the separating comma is. When not recognized as
a quantifier a sequence such as \f(CR{\(rsQ1\(rsE,2}\fP is treated as the literal string
\f(CR{1,2}\fP.
.SS "Non\-printing characters"
.sp
A second use of backslash provides a way of encoding non\-printing characters
in patterns in a visible manner. There is no restriction on the appearance of
non\-printing characters in a pattern, but when a pattern is being prepared by
text editing, it is often easier to use one of the following escape sequences
instead of the binary character it represents. In an ASCII or Unicode
environment, these escapes are as follows:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 21
\f(CR\(rsa\fP
.ad l
alarm, that is, the BEL character (\f(CR0x07\fP)
.TP 21
\f(CR\(rsc\fP\fIx\fP
.ad l
"control\-\fIx\fP", where \fIx\fP is a non\-control ASCII character
.TP 21
\f(CR\(rse\fP
.ad l
escape (\f(CR0x1B\fP)
.TP 21
\f(CR\(rsf\fP
.ad l
form feed (\f(CR0x0C\fP)
.TP 21
\f(CR\(rsn\fP
.ad l
linefeed (\f(CR0x0A\fP)
.TP 21
\f(CR\(rsr\fP
.ad l
carriage return (\f(CR0x0D\fP) (but see below)
.TP 21
\f(CR\(rst\fP
.ad l
tab (\f(CR0x09\fP)
.TP 21
\f(CR\(rs0\fP\fIdd\fP
.ad l
character with octal code 0\fIdd\fP
.TP 21
\f(CR\(rs\fP\fIddd\fP
.ad l
character with octal code \fIddd\fP, or back reference
.TP 21
\f(CR\(rso{\fP\fIddd..\fP\f(CR}\fP
.ad l
character with octal code \fIddd..\fP
.TP 21
\f(CR\(rsx\fP\fIhh\fP
.ad l
character with hex code \fIhh\fP
.TP 21
\f(CR\(rsx{\fP\fIhhh..\fP\f(CR}\fP
.ad l
character with hex code \fIhhh..\fP
.TP 21
\f(CR\(rsN{U+\fP\fIhhh..\fP\f(CR}\fP
.ad l
character with Unicode hex code point \fIhhh..\fP
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
A description of how back references work is given
later,
following the discussion of
parenthesized groups.
.sp
By default, after \f(CR\(rsx\fP that is not followed by {, one or two hexadecimal
digits are read (letters can be in upper or lower case). If the character that
follows \f(CR\(rsx\fP is neither { nor a hexadecimal digit, an error occurs. This is
different from Perl\(cqs default behaviour, which generates a NUL character, but
is in line with the behaviour of Perl\(cqs \*(Aqstrict\*(Aq mode in re.
.sp
Any number of hexadecimal digits may appear between \f(CR\(rsx{\fP and \f(CR}\fP. If a character
other than a hexadecimal digit appears between \f(CR\(rsx{\fP and \f(CR}\fP, or if there is no
terminating \f(CR}\fP, an error occurs.
.sp
Characters whose code points are less than 256 can be defined by either of the
two syntaxes for \f(CR\(rsx\fP or by an octal sequence. There is no difference in the way
they are handled. For example, \f(CR\(rsxdc\fP is exactly the same as \f(CR\(rsx{dc}\fP or \f(CR\(rs334\fP.
However, using the braced versions does make such sequences easier to read.
.sp
Support is available for some ECMAScript (JavaScript) escape sequences via
two compile\-time options. If \fBPCRE2_ALT_BSUX\fP is set, the sequence \f(CR\(rsx\fP followed
by \f(CR{\fP is not recognized. Only if \f(CR\(rsx\fP is followed by two hexadecimal digits is it
recognized as a character escape. Otherwise it is interpreted as a literal \f(CRx\fP
character. In this mode, support for code points greater than 256 is provided
by \f(CR\(rsu\fP, which must be followed by four hexadecimal digits; otherwise it is
interpreted as a literal \f(CRu\fP character.
.sp
\fBPCRE2_EXTRA_ALT_BSUX\fP has the same effect as \fBPCRE2_ALT_BSUX\fP and, in addition,
\f(CR\(rsu{hhh..}\fP is recognized as the character specified by hexadecimal code point.
There may be any number of hexadecimal digits, but unlike other places that
also use curly brackets, spaces are not allowed and would result in the string
being interpreted as a literal. This syntax is from ECMAScript 6.
.sp
The \f(CR\(rsN{U+\fP\fIhhh...\fP\f(CR}\fP escape sequence is recognized only when PCRE2 is operating in
UTF mode. Perl also uses \f(CR\(rsN{\fP\fIname\fP\f(CR}\fP to specify characters by Unicode name; PCRE2
does not support this. Note that when \f(CR\(rsN\fP is not followed by an opening brace
(curly bracket) it has an entirely different meaning, matching any character
that is not a newline.
.sp
There are some legacy applications where the escape sequence \f(CR\(rsr\fP is expected to
match a newline. If the \fBPCRE2_EXTRA_ESCAPED_CR_IS_LF\fP option is set, \f(CR\(rsr\fP in a
pattern is converted to \f(CR\(rsn\fP so that it matches a LF (linefeed) instead of a CR
(carriage return) character.
.sp
An error occurs if \f(CR\(rsc\fP is not followed by a character whose ASCII code point
is in the range 32 to 126. The precise effect of \f(CR\(rsc\fP\fIx\fP is as follows: if \fIx\fP is a
lower case letter, it is converted to upper case. Then bit 6 of the character
(\f(CR0x40\fP) is inverted. Thus \f(CR\(rscA\fP to \f(CR\(rscZ\fP become \f(CR0x01\fP to \f(CR0x1A\fP (A is 41, Z is
5A), but \f(CR\(rsc{\fP becomes \f(CR0x3B\fP ({ is 7B), and \f(CR\(rsc;\fP becomes \f(CR0x7B\fP (; is 3B). If
the code unit following \f(CR\(rsc\fP has a code point less than 32 or greater than 126,
a compile\-time error occurs.
.sp
For differences in the way some escapes behave in EBCDIC environments,
see section EBCDIC ENVIRONMENTS
below.
.SS "Octal escapes and back references"
.sp
The escape \f(CR\(rso\fP must be followed by a sequence of octal digits, enclosed in
braces. An error occurs if this is not the case. This escape provides a way of
specifying character code points as octal numbers greater than 0777, and it
also allows octal numbers and backreferences to be unambiguously distinguished.
.sp
If braces are not used, after \f(CR\(rs0\fP up to two further octal digits are read.
However, if the \fBPCRE2_EXTRA_NO_BS0\fP option is set, at least one more octal digit
must follow \f(CR\(rs0\fP (use \f(CR\(rs00\fP to generate a NUL character). Make sure you supply
two digits after the initial zero if the pattern character that follows is
itself an octal digit.
.sp
Inside a character class, when a backslash is followed by any octal digit, up
to three octal digits are read to generate a code point. Any subsequent digits
stand for themselves. The sequences \f(CR\(rs8\fP and \f(CR\(rs9\fP are treated as the literal
characters \f(CR8\fP and \f(CR9\fP.
.sp
Outside a character class, Perl\(cqs handling of a backslash followed by a digit
other than 0 is complicated by ambiguity, and Perl has changed over time,
causing PCRE2 also to change. From PCRE2 release 10.45 there is an option
called \fBPCRE2_EXTRA_PYTHON_OCTAL\fP that causes PCRE2 to use Python\(cqs unambiguous
rules. The next two subsections describe the two sets of rules.
.sp
For greater clarity and unambiguity, it is best to avoid following backslash by a
digit greater than zero. Instead, use \f(CR\(rso{...}\fP or \f(CR\(rsx{...}\fP
to specify numerical
character code points, and \f(CR\(rsg{...}\fP to specify backreferences.
.SS "Perl rules for non\-class backslash 1\-9"
.sp
All the digits that follow the backslash are read as a decimal number. If the
number is less than 10, begins with the digit 8 or 9, or if there are at least
that many previous capture groups in the expression, the entire sequence is
taken as a back reference. Otherwise, up to three octal digits are read to form
a character code. For example:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rs040\fP
.ad l
is another way of writing an ASCII space
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rs40\fP
.ad l
is the same, provided there are fewer than 40 previous capture groups
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rs7\fP
.ad l
is always a backreference
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rs11\fP
.ad l
might be a backreference, or another way of writing a tab
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rs011\fP
.ad l
is always a tab
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rs0113\fP
.ad l
is a tab followed by the character "3"
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rs113\fP
.ad l
might be a backreference, otherwise the character with octal code 113
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rs377\fP
.ad l
might be a backreference, otherwise the value 255 (decimal)
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rs81\fP
.ad l
is always a backreference
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
Note that octal values of 100 or greater that are specified using this syntax
must not be introduced by a leading zero, because no more than three octal
digits are ever read.
.SS "Python rules for non\-class backslash 1\-9"
.sp
If there are at least three octal digits after the backslash, exactly three are
read as an octal code point number, but the value must be no greater than
377, even in modes where higher code point values are supported. Any
subsequent digits stand for themselves. If there are fewer than three octal
digits, the sequence is taken as a decimal back reference. Thus, for example,
\f(CR\(rs12\fP is always a back reference, independent of how many captures there are in
the pattern. An error is generated for a reference to a non\-existent capturing
group.
.SS "Constraints on character values"
.sp
Characters that are specified using octal or hexadecimal numbers are
limited to certain values, as follows:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 20
8\-bit non\-UTF mode
.ad l
No greater than \f(CR0xff\fP
.TP 20
16\-bit non\-UTF mode
.ad l
No greater than \f(CR0xffff\fP
.TP 20
32\-bit non\-UTF mode
.ad l
No greater than \f(CR0xffffffff\fP
.TP 20
All UTF modes
.ad l
No greater than \f(CR0x10ffff\fP and a valid code point
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
Invalid Unicode code points are all those in the range \f(CR0xd800\fP to \f(CR0xdfff\fP (the
so\-called "surrogate" code points). The check for these can be disabled by the
caller of pcre2_compile() by setting the option
\fBPCRE2_EXTRA_ALLOW_SURROGATE_ESCAPES\fP. However, this is possible only in UTF\-8
and UTF\-32 modes, because these values are not representable in UTF\-16.
.SS "Escape sequences in character classes"
.sp
All the sequences that define a single character value can be used both inside
and outside character classes. In addition, inside a character class, \f(CR\(rsb\fP is
interpreted as the backspace character (\f(CR0x08\fP).
.sp
When not followed by an opening brace, \f(CR\(rsN\fP is not allowed in a character class.
\f(CR\(rsB\fP, \f(CR\(rsR\fP, and \f(CR\(rsX\fP are not special inside a character class. Like other
unrecognized alphabetic escape sequences, they cause an error. Outside a
character class, these sequences have different meanings.
.SS "Unsupported escape sequences"
.sp
In Perl, the sequences \f(CR\(rsF\fP, \f(CR\(rsl\fP, \f(CR\(rsL\fP, \f(CR\(rsu\fP, and \f(CR\(rsU\fP are recognized by its string
handler and used to modify the case of following characters. By default, PCRE2
does not support these escape sequences in patterns. However, if either of the
\fBPCRE2_ALT_BSUX\fP or \fBPCRE2_EXTRA_ALT_BSUX\fP options is set, \f(CR\(rsU\fP matches a \f(CRU\fP
character, and \f(CR\(rsu\fP can be used to define a character by code point, as
described above.
.SS "Absolute and relative backreferences"
.sp
The sequence \f(CR\(rsg\fP followed by a signed or unsigned number, optionally enclosed
in braces, is an absolute or relative backreference. A named backreference
can be coded as \f(CR\(rsg{\fP\fIname\fP\f(CR}\fP. Backreferences are discussed
later,
following the discussion of
parenthesized groups.
.SS "Absolute and relative subroutine calls"
.sp
For compatibility with Oniguruma, the non\-Perl syntax \f(CR\(rsg\fP followed by a name or
a number enclosed either in angle brackets or single quotes, is an alternative
syntax for referencing a capture group as a subroutine. Details are discussed
later.
Note that \f(CR\(rsg{...}\fP (Perl syntax) and \f(CR\(rsg<...>\fP (Oniguruma syntax) are \fInot\fP
synonymous. The former is a backreference; the latter is a
subroutine
call.
.SS "Generic character types"
.sp
Another use of backslash is for specifying generic character types:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rsd\fP
.ad l
Any decimal digit.
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rsD\fP
.ad l
Any character that is not a decimal digit.
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rsh\fP
.ad l
Any horizontal white space character.
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rsH\fP
.ad l
Any character that is not a horizontal white space character.
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rsN\fP
.ad l
Any character that is not a newline.
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rss\fP
.ad l
Any white space character.
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rsS\fP
.ad l
Any character that is not a white space character.
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rsv\fP
.ad l
Any vertical white space character.
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rsV\fP
.ad l
Any character that is not a vertical white space character.
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rsw\fP
.ad l
Any "word" character.
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rsW\fP
.ad l
Any "non\-word" character.
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
The \f(CR\(rsN\fP escape sequence has the same meaning as
the \f(CR.\fP metacharacter
when \fBPCRE2_DOTALL\fP is not set, but setting \fBPCRE2_DOTALL\fP does not change the
meaning of \f(CR\(rsN\fP. Note that when \f(CR\(rsN\fP is followed by an opening brace it has a
different meaning. See the section Non\-printing characters
above for details. Perl also uses \f(CR\(rsN{\fP\fIname\fP\f(CR}\fP to specify characters by Unicode
name; PCRE2 does not support this.
.sp
Each pair of lower and upper case escape sequences partitions the complete set
of characters into two disjoint sets. Any given character matches one, and only
one, of each pair. The sequences can appear both inside and outside character
classes. They each match one character of the appropriate type. If the current
matching point is at the end of the subject string, all of them fail, because
there is no character to match.
.sp
The default \f(CR\(rss\fP characters are HT (9), LF (10), VT (11), FF (12), CR (13), and
space (32), which are defined as white space in the "C" locale. This list may
vary if locale\-specific matching is taking place. For example, in some locales
the "non\-breaking space" character (\f(CR0xA0\fP) is recognized as white space, and in
others the VT character is not.
.sp
A "word" character is an underscore or any character that is a letter or digit.
By default, the definition of letters and digits is controlled by PCRE2\(cqs
low\-valued character tables, and may vary if locale\-specific matching is taking
place (see
"Locale support"
in the
\f(CRpcre2api\fP
page). For example, in a French locale such as "fr_FR" in Unix\-like systems,
or "french" in Windows, some character codes greater than 127 are used for
accented letters, and these are then matched by \f(CR\(rsw\fP. The use of locales with
Unicode is discouraged.
.sp
By default, characters whose code points are greater than 127 never match \f(CR\(rsd\fP,
\f(CR\(rss\fP, or \f(CR\(rsw\fP, and always match \f(CR\(rsD\fP, \f(CR\(rsS\fP, and \f(CR\(rsW\fP, although this may be different
for characters in the range 128\(em255 when locale\-specific matching is happening.
These escape sequences retain their original meanings from before Unicode
support was available, mainly for efficiency reasons. If the \fBPCRE2_UCP\fP option
is set, the behaviour is changed so that Unicode properties are used to
determine character types, as follows:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rsd\fP
.ad l
Any character that matches \f(CR\(rsp{Nd}\fP (decimal digit).
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rss\fP
.ad l
Any character that matches \f(CR\(rsp{Z}\fP, \f(CR\(rsh\fP, or \f(CR\(rsv\fP.
.TP 7
\f(CR\(rsw\fP
.ad l
Any character that matches \f(CR\(rsp{L}\fP, \f(CR\(rsp{N}\fP, \f(CR\(rsp{Mn}\fP, or \f(CR\(rsp{Pc}\fP.
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
The addition of \f(CR\(rsp{Mn}\fP (non\-spacing mark) and the replacement of an explicit
test for underscore with a test for \f(CR\(rsp{Pc}\fP (connector punctuation) happened in
PCRE2 release 10.43. This brings PCRE2 into line with Perl.
.sp
The upper case escapes match the inverse sets of characters. Note that \f(CR\(rsd\fP
matches only decimal digits, whereas \f(CR\(rsw\fP matches any Unicode digit, as well as
other character categories. Note also that \fBPCRE2_UCP\fP affects \f(CR\(rsb\fP, and
\f(CR\(rsB\fP because they are defined in terms of \f(CR\(rsw\fP and \f(CR\(rsW\fP. Matching these sequences
is noticeably slower when \fBPCRE2_UCP\fP is set.
.sp
The effect of \fBPCRE2_UCP\fP on any one of these escape sequences can be negated by
the options \fBPCRE2_EXTRA_ASCII_BSD\fP, \fBPCRE2_EXTRA_ASCII_BSS\fP, and
\fBPCRE2_EXTRA_ASCII_BSW\fP, respectively. These options can be set and reset within
a pattern by means of an internal option setting
(see below).
.sp
The sequences \f(CR\(rsh\fP, \f(CR\(rsH\fP, \f(CR\(rsv\fP, and \f(CR\(rsV\fP, in contrast to the other sequences, which
match only ASCII characters by default, always match a specific list of code
points, whether or not \fBPCRE2_UCP\fP is set. The horizontal space characters are:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 7
\f(CRU+0009\fP
.ad l
Horizontal tab (HT)
.TP 7
\f(CRU+0020\fP
.ad l
Space
.TP 7
\f(CRU+00A0\fP
.ad l
Non\-break space
.TP 7
\f(CRU+1680\fP
.ad l
Ogham space mark
.TP 7
\f(CRU+180E\fP
.ad l
Mongolian vowel separator
.TP 7
\f(CRU+2000\fP
.ad l
En quad
.TP 7
\f(CRU+2001\fP
.ad l
Em quad
.TP 7
\f(CRU+2002\fP
.ad l
En space
.TP 7
\f(CRU+2003\fP
.ad l
Em space
.TP 7
\f(CRU+2004\fP
.ad l
Three\-per\-em space
.TP 7
\f(CRU+2005\fP
.ad l
Four\-per\-em space
.TP 7
\f(CRU+2006\fP
.ad l
Six\-per\-em space
.TP 7
\f(CRU+2007\fP
.ad l
Figure space
.TP 7
\f(CRU+2008\fP
.ad l
Punctuation space
.TP 7
\f(CRU+2009\fP
.ad l
Thin space
.TP 7
\f(CRU+200A\fP
.ad l
Hair space
.TP 7
\f(CRU+202F\fP
.ad l
Narrow no\-break space
.TP 7
\f(CRU+205F\fP
.ad l
Medium mathematical space
.TP 7
\f(CRU+3000\fP
.ad l
Ideographic space
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
The vertical space characters are:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 7
\f(CRU+000A\fP
.ad l
Linefeed (LF)
.TP 7
\f(CRU+000B\fP
.ad l
Vertical tab (VT)
.TP 7
\f(CRU+000C\fP
.ad l
Form feed (FF)
.TP 7
\f(CRU+000D\fP
.ad l
Carriage return (CR)
.TP 7
\f(CRU+0085\fP
.ad l
Next line (NEL)
.TP 7
\f(CRU+2028\fP
.ad l
Line separator
.TP 7
\f(CRU+2029\fP
.ad l
Paragraph separator
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
In 8\-bit, non\-UTF\-8 mode, only the characters with code points less than 256
are relevant.
.SS "Newline sequences"
.sp
Outside a character class, by default, the escape sequence \f(CR\(rsR\fP matches any
Unicode newline sequence. In 8\-bit non\-UTF\-8 mode \f(CR\(rsR\fP is equivalent to the
following:
.sp
.ss \n[.ss]
.nf
.fam C
(?>\(rsr\(rsn|\(rsn|\(rsx0b|\(rsf|\(rsr|\(rsx85)
.fam
.fi
.ss \n[.ss] 0
.sp
This is an example of an "atomic group", details of which are given
below.
This particular group matches either the two\-character sequence CR followed by
LF, or one of the single characters LF (linefeed, \f(CRU+000A\fP), VT (vertical tab,
\f(CRU+000B\fP), FF (form feed, \f(CRU+000C\fP), CR (carriage return, \f(CRU+000D\fP), or NEL (next
line, \f(CRU+0085\fP). Because this is an atomic group, the two\-character sequence is
treated as a single unit that cannot be split.
.sp
In other modes, two additional characters whose code points are greater than 255
are added: LS (line separator, \f(CRU+2028\fP) and PS (paragraph separator, \f(CRU+2029\fP).
Unicode support is not needed for these characters to be recognized.
.sp
It is possible to restrict \f(CR\(rsR\fP to match only CR, LF, or CRLF (instead of the
complete set of Unicode line endings) by setting the option \fBPCRE2_BSR_ANYCRLF\fP
at compile time. (BSR is an abbreviation for "backslash R".) This can be made
the default when PCRE2 is built; if this is the case, the other behaviour can
be requested via the \fBPCRE2_BSR_UNICODE\fP option. It is also possible to specify
these settings by starting a pattern string with one of the following
sequences:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 15
\f(CR(*BSR_ANYCRLF)\fP
.ad l
CR, LF, or CRLF only.
.TP 15
\f(CR(*BSR_UNICODE)\fP
.ad l
Any Unicode newline sequence.
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
These override the default and the options given to the compiling function.
Note that these special settings, which are not Perl\-compatible, are recognized
only at the very start of a pattern, and that they must be in upper case. If
more than one of them is present, the last one is used. They can be combined
with a change of newline convention; for example, a pattern can start with:
.sp
.ss \n[.ss]
.nf
.fam C
(*ANY)(*BSR_ANYCRLF)
.fam
.fi
.ss \n[.ss] 0
.sp
They can also be combined with the \f(CR(*UTF)\fP or \f(CR(*UCP)\fP special sequences. Inside a
character class, \f(CR\(rsR\fP is treated as an unrecognized escape sequence, and causes
an error.
.SS "Unicode character properties"
.sp
When PCRE2 is built with Unicode support (the default), three additional escape
sequences that match characters with specific properties are available. They
can be used in any mode, though in 8\-bit and 16\-bit non\-UTF modes these
sequences are of course limited to testing characters whose code points are
less than \f(CRU+0100\fP or \f(CRU+10000\fP, respectively. In 32\-bit non\-UTF mode, code points
greater than \f(CR0x10ffff\fP (the Unicode limit) may be encountered. These are all
treated as being in the Unknown script and with an unassigned type.
.sp
Matching characters by Unicode property is not fast, because PCRE2 has to do a
multistage table lookup in order to find a character\(cqs property. That is why
the traditional escape sequences such as \f(CR\(rsd\fP and \f(CR\(rsw\fP do not use Unicode
properties in PCRE2 by default, though you can make them do so by setting the
\fBPCRE2_UCP\fP option or by starting the pattern with \f(CR(*UCP)\fP.
.sp
The extra escape sequences that provide property support are:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 13
\f(CR\(rsp{\fP\fIxx\fP\f(CR}\fP
.ad l
a character with the \fIxx\fP property
.TP 13
\f(CR\(rsP{\fP\fIxx\fP\f(CR}\fP
.ad l
a character without the \fIxx\fP property
.TP 13
\f(CR\(rsX\fP
.ad l
a Unicode extended grapheme cluster
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
For compatibility with Perl, negation can be specified by including a
circumflex between the opening brace and the property. For example, \f(CR\(rsp{^Lu}\fP is
the same as \f(CR\(rsP{Lu}\fP.
.sp
In accordance with Unicode\(cqs "loose matching" rules, ASCII white space
characters, hyphens, and underscores are ignored in the properties represented
by \fIxx\fP above. As well as the space character, ASCII white space can be
tab, linefeed, vertical tab, formfeed, or carriage return.
.sp
Some properties are specified as a name only; others as a name and a value,
separated by a colon or an equals sign. The names and values consist of ASCII
letters and digits (with one Perl\-specific exception, see below). They are not
case sensitive. Note, however, that the escapes themselves, \f(CR\(rsp\fP and \f(CR\(rsP\fP,
\fIare\fP case sensitive. There are abbreviations for many names. The following
examples are all equivalent:
.sp
.ss \n[.ss]
.nf
.fam C
\(rsp{bidiclass=al}
\(rsp{BC=al}
\(rsp{ Bidi_Class : AL }
\(rsp{ Bi\-di class = Al }
\(rsP{ ^ Bi\-di class = Al }
.fam
.fi
.ss \n[.ss] 0
.sp
There is support for Unicode script names, Unicode general category properties,
"Any", which matches any character (including newline), Bidi_Class, a number of
binary (yes/no) properties, and some special PCRE2 properties (described
below).
Certain other Perl properties such as "InMusicalSymbols" are not supported by
PCRE2. Note that \f(CR\(rsP{Any}\fP does not match any characters, so always causes a
match failure.
.SS "Script properties for \f(CR\(rsp\fP and \f(CR\(rsP\fP"
.sp
There are three different syntax forms for matching a script. Each Unicode
character has a basic script and, optionally, a list of other scripts ("Script
Extensions") with which it is commonly used. Using the Adlam script as an
example, \f(CR\(rsp{sc:Adlam}\fP matches characters whose basic script is Adlam, whereas
\f(CR\(rsp{scx:Adlam}\fP matches, in addition, characters that have Adlam in their
extensions list. The full names "script" and "script extensions" for the
property types are recognized and, as for all property specifications, an
equals sign is an alternative to the colon. If a script name is given without a
property type, for example, \f(CR\(rsp{Adlam}\fP, it is treated as \f(CR\(rsp{scx:Adlam}\fP. Perl
changed to this interpretation at release 5.26 and PCRE2 changed at release
10.40.
.sp
Unassigned characters (and in non\-UTF 32\-bit mode, characters with code points
greater than \f(CR0x10FFFF\fP) are assigned the "Unknown" script. Others that are not
part of an identified script are lumped together as "Common". The current list
of recognized script names and their 4\-character abbreviations can be obtained
by running this command:
.sp
.ss \n[.ss]
.nf
.fam C
pcre2test \-LS
.fam
.fi
.ss \n[.ss] 0
.SS "The general category property for \f(CR\(rsp\fP and \f(CR\(rsP\fP"
.sp
Each character has exactly one Unicode general category property, specified by
a two\-letter abbreviation. If only one letter is specified with \f(CR\(rsp\fP or \f(CR\(rsP\fP, it
includes all the general category properties that start with that letter. In
this case, in the absence of negation, the curly brackets in the escape
sequence are optional; these two examples have the same effect:
.sp
.ss \n[.ss]
.nf
.fam C
\(rsp{L}
\(rspL
.fam
.fi
.ss \n[.ss] 0
.sp
The following general category property codes are supported:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 7
\f(CRC\fP
.ad l
Other
.TP 7
\f(CRCc\fP
.ad l
Control
.TP 7
\f(CRCf\fP
.ad l
Format
.TP 7
\f(CRCn\fP
.ad l
Unassigned
.TP 7
\f(CRCo\fP
.ad l
Private use
.TP 7
\f(CRCs\fP
.ad l
Surrogate
.TP 7
\f(CRL\fP
.ad l
Letter
.TP 7
\f(CRLc\fP
.ad l
Cased letter
.TP 7
\f(CRLl\fP
.ad l
Lower case letter
.TP 7
\f(CRLm\fP
.ad l
Modifier letter
.TP 7
\f(CRLo\fP
.ad l
Other letter
.TP 7
\f(CRLt\fP
.ad l
Title case letter
.TP 7
\f(CRLu\fP
.ad l
Upper case letter
.TP 7
\f(CRM\fP
.ad l
Mark
.TP 7
\f(CRMc\fP
.ad l
Spacing mark
.TP 7
\f(CRMe\fP
.ad l
Enclosing mark
.TP 7
\f(CRMn\fP
.ad l
Non\-spacing mark
.TP 7
\f(CRN\fP
.ad l
Number
.TP 7
