.sp
Perl originally used the name \f(CRL&\fP for the \f(CRLc\fP property. This is still supported
by Perl, but discouraged. PCRE2 also still supports it. This property matches
any character that has the \f(CRLu\fP, \f(CRLl\fP, or \f(CRLt\fP property, in other words, any letter
that is not classified as a modifier or "other". From release 10.45 of PCRE2
the properties \f(CRLu\fP, \f(CRLl\fP, and \f(CRLt\fP are all treated as \f(CRLc\fP when case\-independent
matching is set by the \fBPCRE2_CASELESS\fP option or \f(CR(?i)\fP within the pattern. The
other properties are not affected by caseless matching.
.sp
The \f(CRCs\fP (Surrogate) property applies only to characters whose code points are in
the range \f(CRU+D800\fP to \f(CRU+DFFF\fP. These characters are no different to any other
character when PCRE2 is not in UTF mode (using the 16\-bit or 32\-bit library).
However, they are not valid in Unicode strings and so cannot be tested by PCRE2
in UTF mode, unless UTF validity checking has been turned off (see the
discussion of \fBPCRE2_NO_UTF_CHECK\fP in the
\f(CRpcre2api\fP
page).
.sp
The long synonyms for property names that Perl supports (such as \f(CR\(rsp{Letter}\fP)
are not supported by PCRE2, nor is it permitted to prefix any of these
properties with "Is".
.sp
No character that is in the Unicode table has the Cn (unassigned) property.
Instead, this property is assumed for any code point that is not in the
Unicode table.
.SS "Binary (yes/no) properties for \f(CR\(rsp\fP and \f(CR\(rsP\fP"
.sp
Unicode defines a number of binary properties, that is, properties whose only
values are true or false. You can obtain a list of those that are recognized by
\f(CR\(rsp\fP and \f(CR\(rsP\fP, along with their abbreviations, by running this command:
.sp
.ss \n[.ss]
.nf
.fam C
pcre2test \-LP
.fam
.fi
.ss \n[.ss] 0
.SS "The Bidi_Class property for \f(CR\(rsp\fP and \f(CR\(rsP\fP"
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 21
\f(CR\(rsp{Bidi_Class:\fP\fIclass\fP\f(CR}\fP
.ad l
Matches a character with the given class.
.TP 21
